WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.090
Take a fixed length of fence and make a rectangle.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.350
A long thin rectangle encloses very little.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.240
As the sides become more equal, the area grows.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.850
Balanced parts give bigger products.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.050
You could discover this by trying examples.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.840
But in a competition you need to know it is always true,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.010
and what to do when perfect balance is not allowed.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.230
Two positive whole numbers add to twelve.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.590
What is the largest their product can be?

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.450
Pause and try a few pairs.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:34.710
One and eleven give eleven.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.490
Two and ten give twenty.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.490
Three and nine, twenty-seven.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.350
Four and eight, thirty-two.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.510
Five and seven, thirty-five.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.860
Six and six give thirty-six.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.310
The product climbs as the numbers get closer.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:49.930
Here is why.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.930
Write the two numbers as six minus d and six plus d.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.190
Start with a six by six square.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.890
Move a strip from one side to the other.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.200
The pieces no longer fit: a small square of side

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.090
d is missing.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.120
So the product is thirty-six minus d squared.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.450
The further from balance, the more you lose.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.630
Now add a restriction: the two numbers

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:13.580
must be different.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.080
Six and six is no longer allowed.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:18.770
Choose the most balanced pair that is allowed,

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.240
five and seven, giving thirty-five.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.620
The number of parts matters too.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.980
Three fours give a product of sixty-four.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:29.140
Four threes give eighty-one.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.550
Both splits are balanced within their own number of

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.820
parts, but the second product is bigger.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.720
Check how many parts the question actually allows.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.180
For two positive parts with a fixed total,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.880
the most balanced pair gives the largest product.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.290
When a rule forbids perfect balance, get as close as

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:48.080
the rule allows.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.580
Notebook challenge: a rectangle with whole-number sides

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.000
has perimeter twenty-two.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.700
What is its largest possible area?
